2. BFS + Shortest Distance

For an unweighted graph, BFS gives the shortest distance.

Add:  int[] level = new int[n + 1];

Code:

void bfs(int start, ArrayList<Integer>[] graph, int n) {

    boolean[] visited = new boolean[n + 1];
    int[] level = new int[n + 1];

    Queue<Integer> q = new LinkedList<>();

    q.add(start);
    visited[start] = true;
    level[start] = 0;

    while(!q.isEmpty()) {
        int node = q.poll();
        for(int neighbour : graph[node]) {
            if(!visited[neighbour]) {
                visited[neighbour] = true;
                level[neighbour] = level[node] + 1;
                q.add(neighbour);
            }
        }
    }
}


Remember: level[neighbour] = level[node] + 1;
means: Neighbour is one edge farther from the source.

So:

level[source] = 0
level[neighbours] = 1
level[their neighbours] = 2
...
